{
  "id": "80771",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Properties of the Transition Elements",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The transition elements have many properties in common with other metals. They are almost all hard, high-melting solids that conduct heat and electricity well. They readily form alloys and lose electrons to form stable cations. In addition, transition metals form a wide variety of stable "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "coordination compounds"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A stable substance consisting of a central metal atom or ion (acting as a Lewis acid) surrounded by molecules or ions (ligands) that donate pairs of electrons (acting as Lewis bases) through coordinate covalent bonds."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1805572049",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "367947667",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", in which the central metal atom or ion acts as a Lewis acid and accepts one or more pairs of electrons. Many different molecules and ions can donate lone pairs to the metal center, serving as Lewis bases. In this module, we focus primarily on the chemical behavior of the elements of the first transition series."
              }
            ],
            "id": "920221087",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Transition metals demonstrate a wide range of chemical properties. The variation in reduction potentials (see "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Standard Reduction Potentials"
                  }
                ],
                "href": "/course/link/iz1ln_standard_reduction_potentials_xdwka",
                "idref": "80670",
                "target": "self",
                "type": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": " in the appendix) shows that some transition metals are strong reducing agents, and others are relatively unreactive. For example, the lanthanides all form stable 3+ aqueous cations from oxidation of the three valence electrons in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals (these electrons have small ionization energies). The driving force for such oxidations is similar to that of alkaline earth metals, such as Be or Mg, forming Be"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and Mg"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " by losing their two valence electrons from the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbital. On the other hand, materials like platinum and gold have higher reduction potentials, meaning they are less easily oxidized. Platinum's and gold’s ability to resist oxidation from various substances makes them useful materials for durable, long-lasting applications such as constructing circuits and jewelry."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2707147669",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Ions of the lighter "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block elements, such as Cr"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", Fe"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and Co"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", form colorful hydrated ions that are stable in water. However, ions in the period just below these (Mo"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", Ru"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and Ir"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are unstable and react readily with oxygen from the air. Most simple, water-stable ions formed by the heavier "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block elements are oxyanions, such as MoO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2− "
              },
              {
                "text": "and ReO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "752386870",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Transition metals can form compounds with a wide range of oxidation states. Some of the observed oxidation states of the elements of the first transition series are shown below. Moving from left to right across the first transition series, we see that the number of common oxidation states increases at first to a maximum toward the middle of the table, then decreases. The values in the table are typical values; there are other known values, and it is possible to synthesize new additions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "25179244",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Transition metals of the first transition series can form compounds with varying oxidation states."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2091120468",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4fbyz7u4qks6m3b",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "123031472",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/3A/3A6248AB8C96E2D965CC8AB40AEA04AF/trans_metals_oxid_table.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2693512775",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "210373104",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For the elements scandium through manganese (the first half of the first transition series), the highest oxidation state corresponds to the loss of all of the electrons in both the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals of their valence shells. The titanium(IV) ion, for example, is formed when the titanium atom loses its two 3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d "
              },
              {
                "text": "and two 4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": " electrons. These highest oxidation states are the most stable forms of scandium, titanium, and vanadium. However, it is not possible to continue to remove all of the valence electrons from metals as we continue through the series. Iron is known to form oxidation states from +2 to +6, with iron(II) and iron(III) being the most common. Most of the elements of the first transition series form ions with a charge of +2 or +3 that are stable in water, although those of the early members of the series can be readily oxidized by air."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3520609034",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "3388493386",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Compare the oxidation properties of the following transition metals: Ti, Fe, Co. Which transition metal has the highest maximum possible oxidation state? Which transition metal can most easily achieve its maximum possible oxidation state?"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1926463198",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Solution"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2502978769",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The transition metal that has the most valence electrons will have the highest maximum possible oxidation state because it has the most electrons that can be lost. Comparing the electron configuration of each element, we see that cobalt (Co) has a configuration of [Kr]4s"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "3d"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "7"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " with nine (two "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "s"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and seven "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " electrons) valence electrons, so in theory it has the highest maximum possible oxidation state of +9. Titanium (Ti) [Kr]4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "s"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " has four valence electrons, so its maximum oxidation state is +4, and iron (Fe) [Kr]4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "s"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " has eight valence electrons, so its maximum oxidation state is +8."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2655716585",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "However, in practice, the transition metal that can most easily achieve its maximum oxidation state is titanium (Ti). As electronegativity of elements increases from left to right across the periodic table, Ti is less electronegative (more easily oxidized) than Fe or Co, which are more electronegative and less likely to lose electrons (less easily oxidized). Titanium is frequently found in its maximum oxidation state of +4 as Ti"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "4+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion in compounds, whereas Fe and Co ions are usually found in lower oxidation states of +2 or +3."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1853195802",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "170892775",
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
