          }
        ],
        "id": "1482234172",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "didigetthis",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The elements of the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "second transition series"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Transition elements in the fifth period of the periodic table (second row of the d-block), atomic numbers 39–47."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3170707503",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2262558221",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " and third transition series generally are more stable in higher oxidation states than are the elements of the first series. In general, the atomic radius increases down a group, which leads to the ions of the second and third series being larger than those in the first series. Removing electrons from orbitals that are located farther from the nucleus is easier than removing electrons close to the nucleus. For example, molybdenum and tungsten, members of group 6, are limited mostly to an oxidation state of +6 in aqueous solution. Chromium, the lightest member of the group, forms stable Cr"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions in water and, in the absence of air, less stable Cr"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions. The sulfide with the highest oxidation state for chromium is Cr"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", which contains the Cr"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ion.  Molybdenum and tungsten form sulfides in which the metals exhibit oxidation states of +4 and +6 because their valence electrons are farther from the nucleus and take less energy to ionize."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2594266961",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "111698283",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Which is the strongest oxidizing agent in acidic solution: dichromate ion, which contains chromium(VI), permanganate ion, which contains manganese(VII), or titanium dioxide, which contains titanium(IV)?"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1552638622",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "First, we need to look up the "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Standard Reduction Potentials"
                      }
                    ],
                    "href": "/course/link/iz1ln_standard_reduction_potentials_xdwka",
                    "idref": "80670",
                    "target": "self",
                    "type": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " for each oxide in the specified oxidation state in acidic solution:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4200014756",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "         "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "72600840",
                    "src": "{\\mathrm{Cr}}_2\\mathrm O_7^{2-}\\;+\\;14\\;\\mathrm H^++\\;6\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\\;+\\;7\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;E^o\\;=\\;+1.33\\;\\mathrm V\\\\\\mathrm{MnO}_4^-\\;+\\;8\\;\\mathrm H^+\\;+\\;5\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;E^o\\;=\\;+1.51\\;\\mathrm V\\\\{\\mathrm{TiO}}_2\\;+\\;4\\;\\mathrm H^-\\;+\\;2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{Ti}^{2+}\\;+\\;2\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;E^o\\;=\\;-0.50\\;\\mathrm V",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ulp6wxe5b65x2ki",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Recall that the higher the reduction potential, the more readily the reactant undergoes reduction and the better (stronger) it is as an oxidizing agent. Permanganate (MnO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") has the highest reduction potential, so it is the strongest oxidizing agent (oxidizer) under acidic conditions. Dichromate (Cr"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "7"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") is next, and titanium dioxide (TiO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") is the weakest oxidizing agent (least readily undergoes reduction) of this set."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2895428959",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "2900568231",
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
